<h3>What is No-Fault Insurance? </h3>
<p>No-Fault Insurance, also known as Personal Injury Protection (PIP) insurance, is a type of car insurance that is required by law in the state of Florida. It pays for medical expenses and lost wages for the driver and passengers in the event of an accident, regardless of who is at fault. </p>
<h3>What are the requirements for No-Fault Insurance in Florida? </h3>
<p>Florida is a no-fault state, which means that in the event of a car accident, each party’s own insurance covers their injuries regardless of who was at fault. Here are the key requirements for No-Fault Insurance in Florida:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Personal Injury Protection (PIP):</strong> All Florida drivers must have Personal Injury Protection insurance included in their car insurance policy<sup>123</sup>. Each policyholder is required to have $10,000 in PIP<sup>3</sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Property Damage Liability (PDL):</strong> Florida requires drivers to carry a minimum of $10,000 in property damage liability coverage<sup>4</sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Bodily Injury Liability (BIL):</strong> While not mandatory for all drivers, those registered as taxis must carry BIL coverage of $125,000 per person and $250,000 per occurrence<sup>5</sup>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Please note, while these are the minimum requirements, many drivers choose to carry higher limits to further protect themselves in case of an accident.</p>
